<title>POLAND: Poland agrees to tighten car import rules - paper.</title>
<headline>Poland agrees to tighten car import rules - paper.</headline>
<dateline>WARSAW 1997-02-01</dateline>
<text>
<p>Poland has agreed to European Commission demands that it tightens rules on imports of parts for car assembly in Poland, Rzeczpospolita newspaper said on Saturday.</p>
<p>It said the EU had been especially critical of terms under which South Korea's Daewoo was allowed to import car kits duty free for assembly in Poland and that Polish negotiators accepted this week stricter EU interpretation of rules guiding such imports.</p>
<p>&quot;Poland agreed to a more rigorous interpretation...of customs regulations, which apply to imports of car parts by Daewoo and other automotive concerns,&quot; Rzeczpospolita quoted a Polish negotiator in Brussels as saying.</p>
<p>Rzeczpospolita said Daewoo has been bringing into Poland duty-free cars knocked-down into eight units and assembling them at their Polish Daewoo-FSO venture, which the EU has argued boiled down to duty-free car imports.</p>
<p>The newspaper said Polish officials and European Commission negotiators would meet next week in Warsaw to present concrete proposals for changes in car import rules.</p>
<p>-- Warsaw Newsroom +48 22 653 9700</p>
